# AlgoQuest — Cursor Project Rules
# These rules guide AI code generation in this project.
# Place this file at: .cursorrules (project root)

## Project Overview
AlgoQuest is an AI-powered LeetCode learning platform. Students learn from scratch (Python basics) to solving hard LeetCode problems, guided by ML that adapts to their skill level.

## Tech Stack
- Frontend: Next.js 14 (App Router), TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Framer Motion
- Backend: FastAPI (Python 3.11+), SQLAlchemy, Alembic
- Database: PostgreSQL via Supabase
- Auth: Supabase Auth
- ML: scikit-learn
- Code execution: Judge0 API

## Color Tokens (always use these CSS variables)
```
--color-bg: #0D0F1A
--color-surface: #161929
--color-accent-purple: #7C3AED
--color-accent-cyan: #06B6D4
--color-success: #10B981
--color-warning: #F59E0B
--color-error: #EF4444
--color-text-primary: #F1F5F9
--color-text-muted: #94A3B8
--color-border: #1E2940
```

## Coding Standards

### TypeScript / React
- All components are functional with TypeScript (no `any` types)
- Use `'use client'` directive only when necessary (prefer server components)
- Props interfaces are always explicitly typed
- Use Zod for all form validation
- Use React Hook Form for all forms
- Use Zustand for global state (no Redux, no Context API for global state)
- Error boundaries on every major page

### Python / FastAPI
- Python 3.11+ type hints everywhere
- Pydantic v2 for all schemas
- Use `async def` for all route handlers
- All ML inference is async (run in thread pool if sync)
- Never expose raw database errors to API responses
- Use loguru for logging (not print statements)

### API Design
- All API routes are prefixed with `/api/v1/`
- Consistent response format:
  ```json
  { "success": true, "data": {...} }
  { "success": false, "error": "message", "code": "ERROR_CODE" }
  ```
- Use HTTP status codes correctly (200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 404, 422, 500)

### Database
- Use Alembic for all schema changes (never edit tables manually)
- All models have `created_at` and `updated_at` timestamps
- Use UUIDs for all primary keys

### ML Models
- All ML models are saved as `.pkl` files in `backend/app/ml/saved_models/`
- Every ML function has a fallback if model is not loaded
- ML predictions are always logged to DB for future retraining

## File Naming
- React components: PascalCase (`ProblemCard.tsx`)
- Utility functions: camelCase (`formatScore.ts`)
- API routes: snake_case in FastAPI (`/api/v1/user_progress`)
- Python files: snake_case (`level_detector.py`)

## Levels
- Beginner: slug `beginner`, color `#10B981` (green)
- Intermediate: slug `intermediate`, color `#06B6D4` (cyan)
- Advanced: slug `advanced`, color `#7C3AED` (purple)

## ML Level-Up Rule
Trigger level-up suggestion when:
- Last 3 submissions all have `score >= 70` AND `passed_all_tests == true`
- User is not already at max level
- User hasn't been shown a level-up prompt in the last 24 hours

## Judge0 Language ID
- Python 3: `71`

## Important UX Rules
- Every problem has exactly 3 progressive hints (cost: 5 XP each)
- "Optimal solution" is revealed only after the student submits
- Scores show: 0-49 (red, "Keep practicing"), 50-74 (amber, "Good effort"), 75-89 (cyan, "Great"), 90-100 (green, "Excellent")
- XP gained per problem: Easy=10, Medium=25, Hard=50. Deduct 5 per hint used.

## Do Not
- Do not use `any` in TypeScript
- Do not use `print()` in Python (use loguru)
- Do not call ML models synchronously in API handlers (use thread pools)
- Do not expose Supabase service role key to frontend
- Do not skip error handling on Judge0 API calls (it can timeout)
